  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 125 AM MDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nIt definitely felt more like autumn when I came into work this \nevening. This was one of the first times I broke out my windbreaker \nsince last spring. An we do have a gusty breeze this evening \nfollowing the passage of a cold front. We still have a few light\nshowers out there this evening, but nothing heavy. \n\nToday looks like a rain free day for a vast majority of the area as \nmuch drier air passes in from the west, dropping precipitable water \nvalues to under a half an inch for most locations. The one exception \nwill be across far southern Wyoming, mainly along and south of \nInterstate 80, where a bit more moisture will linger. We could see \nan isolated shower or thunderstorm here, but the chance is less than \n1 out of 7 and therefore the POPs will be largely silent. Most \nlocations should see a light to moderate wind today. Again, the \nexception will be Sweetwater County, where a gusty breeze will \ncontinue. And as for fire weather concerns, elevated will be likely \nbut humidity should remain above critical levels. Temperatures will \nbe cooler than yesterday, but still remain above normal for most. \nNorthern Wyoming will be a bit cooler though, with places like \nBuffalo and Cody struggling to get above 70. \n\nMoisture begins to increase tonight. Enough mid level moisture may \nreturn later tonight for a few isolated showers in the western \nmountains late tonight. The bulk of the moisture should hold off \nuntil late in the day and especially after sunset. The culprits for \nthis will be a plume of monsoonal moisture moving in from the south, \nimpacting areas largely East of the Divide. At this same time and \nupper level low will be moving northward from Idaho and into western \nMontana, dragging a negatively tilted trough and cold front through \nthe area. And there could be some decent rain in eastern\nportions of the area. For factors for, we will have deep \nmoisture, with precipitable water values approaching 175 percent\nof normal. The trough and cold front will also add some extra \nlift. The main factor against will be steering flow, which looks\nrather strong. There is generally 30 knots at 700 millibars \nwith up to 50 knots at 500 millibars. So, any showers and storms\nwill be moving at a decent pace. The main concern would be \npossibly training storms, or in southwesterly upslope areas, \nlike the southern end of the Bighorn Range. The greatest chance \nwould be largely along and east of a Kaycee to Rock Springs \nline, with lessening chances further north and west. And with \nthe southwest flow ahead of the system, it will be a warm end to\nthe weekend with many locations below 6000 feet well into the \n80s for high temperatures.\n\nMost showers should clear the area by sunrise on Monday, replaced by \nanother cooler and drier airmass, with temperatures falling back \ntoward normal levels. Concerns on this day shift to fire weather \nagain. There will still be a decent height and pressure gradient \nbetween ridging over the southern United States and the upper level \nlow moving into the Prairie Provinces. The result will be a gusty \nwind across much of the area. And with very dry air moving into the \narea, we will likely see at least elevated fire weather. The \nsouthern two thirds of the area; especially East of Rock Springs and \nRiverton, has at least a 1 out of 2 chance of relative humidity \nfalling under 15 percent. We may need some fire weather highlights \nfor this day, it is a bit too early to issue right now though. \n\nTuesday looks mainly dry at this point, although signs are pointing \nto another shot of monsoonal moisture starting to move into southern \nWyoming for late Tuesday as another low moves toward the area. Most \nof the moisture looks to hold off until Wednesday though. A \npreponderance of the guidance takes this low more to the east \nand closer to the Cowboy State, bringing a better chance of \nshowers for Wednesday and into Thursday and possibly into Friday\nas well. Details of precipitation amounts and timing of the \nheaviest precipitation remain in flux though as there are \ndifferent solutions in regards to the exact timing and placement\nof the low. Nevertheless, we are looking at an increased chance\nof showers for later in the week.",
